plan EntityDomains
title   Entity domains: coherent records, conservative repair and honest predictions
status  proposed
goal
    Plain operations get a declared shared-data meaning without author-written cache maintenance.
    One Runtime store keeps record fields coherent across domains. Machines own workflow; the
    domain records places, dependencies, predictions and server effects. Repair is conservative,
    bounded and explainable, and never predicts list membership. Operations are
    @plan.Operations.O1, hydration @plan.ServerRendering.O5 and Stories @plan.Testing.O1.
outcomes
    O1  Domains, records, repair and predictions
        An operation describes a request; a domain describes what it means for shared data.
        Terms: a record is one entity instance by key; an answer is one read entry's result for
        one input; a delta is a change to a record's confirmed base; a position orders sources by
        when their request started and landed; an answer's frontier is the newest position it
        reflects; a carrier is an active answer or loader whose shape holds a field.
        domain(id, entries) lists read(resource), write(transaction) and listen(stream) entries.
        An operation belongs to at most one domain and machines reach it only as O.Domain.entry,
        declared under operations by a definition, or by a fragment for its actor; operation
        modules never import entities. Domains never send, commit or choose a state. A read needs
        an output Schema. An operation registered twice (in two domains, or two entries of one) or
        two byKey loaders for one entity throw InvalidConfiguration at App construction or lazy
        adoption, for domains built by factories as for literal ones (a factory parameterises its
        domain, entity and operation ids); flow check reports them too, and warns on an
        unregistered operation whose output carries an entity's key fields. The domain owns
        shared data (the prediction, its confirmation and rollback, repair, the record queue,
        uncertain and unconfirmed); the operation and the machine own workflow (which event each
        ending becomes, its route, retry and cancel). Memory never holds a copy of a record field;
        views read records and answers.

        entity({ id, fields, primaryKey, version? }) declares fields, key and the per-record
        version field (version optional: the runner's ruling, since unversioned entities are a
        stated case with a flow check warning). One entity object per id; a duplicate is a
        construction error. A read entry is built as read(resource) refined by methods:
        .one(Entity, at?) holds one record, .list(Entity, at, member?) holds records along a path,
        Entity.pick names an explicit shape,
        .byKey(...) marks the record loader, .gone(tag) (only with .byKey; GoneWithoutKey,
        proposed name, otherwise) turns that failure tag into a tombstone, and
        .derivedFrom(Entity, fields?) declares values computed from records the response does not
        hold; omitted fields means any field, an empty list means membership only. Conditions
        (where) use eq, oneOf, contains, between, overlaps, gt, gte, lt, lte, isNull, and, or, not
        and serverMatches over record fields, input fields and constants. Each answers yes, no or
        maybe: maybe means a field not carried, a value the operator cannot use, or an answer the
        server decides (serverMatches, null order, an empty list, a present null input, an absent
        input outside and, a Date tie at the millisecond). Ordering takes numbers and Dates,
        compared with Effect's Order.Number after a finiteness check and Order.Date. A condition
        about the signed-in account uses an input field the machine supplies, or serverMatches
        when only the server applies it. An answer's input is the Type side the machine passed,
        never decoded again; an Encoded input is decoded once at its boundary, where a decoding
        default fills a missing key, and a failure makes the answer maybe with InputDecodeFailed.
        A Type-side optional field with no default, when absent, drops its constraint directly
        under and or as the whole condition, and only its own bound for between and overlaps;
        elsewhere it is maybe. Every list or derived input must appear in where, orderBy, pagedBy,
        ignoring or serverMatches, or construction fails; serverMatches names inputs whose
        filtering only the server knows.

        Order: orderBy ends with every entity key. A list is ordered, unordered or server-ranked
        (optionally narrowed by unaffectedBy). Order is the server's claim and the client never
        sorts. Each response is checked on its own number, boolean, date and key-field values,
        never strings, whose collation only the server knows, comparing with Effect's Order; a pair
        whose sort key is null, non-finite, an invalid Date or a Date tie is skipped, never
        HiddenOrder; a conflict raises HiddenOrder naming the records, and that answer is treated
        as server-ranked for the rest of the Runtime. pagedBy(input, next) declares paging;
        entry.more loads the next page and the lane exposes hasMore. Repair re-fetches from page
        one up to the loaded count and publishes the whole chain at once, serving the old chain
        stale until then. A failed page keeps the previous chain, stale with the failure; a
        matching change mid-rebuild restarts it from page one. Extra list metadata has no repair
        guarantee (flow check warns); model totals as derived reads.

        Declarations are recorded once at construction; predicts records its entities, keys and
        field names, and a field's function is called with the record beneath and the real input
        at write start and on each replay. A recording parameter read after its callback returns,
        or enumerated, is an error. A field's function must be pure, deterministic and must not
        write to its arguments; store records and inputs are frozen, a write to them is a
        prediction fault, and the checkReplays Runtime option (on in Stories and propertyTest)
        calls it twice and compares. flow check requires each function passed to a recorded
        position to be one expression in the allowlist grammar (@plan.CommandLine.O5).

        Changes: predicts returns Entity.patch(key, fields) or Entity.delete(key, { version? });
        Entity.patch is for predicts only. Patch fields are recorded reads, constants or functions
        (beneath, input) => value; a key is recorded reads and constants; an array key (from the
        input Schema) is recorded as many and predicts each listed record once. Entity.patch,
        Entity.delete, Entity.fields and .byKey take any value carrying the key's fields, or an
        array of them; the parameter is the key type, so a fresh literal with an extra field is a
        type error; Flow keeps only the primaryKey fields. A patch may only touch an entity named
        in a place or alsoChanges target of that entry; version fields cannot be patched, filtered
        or ordered by, so a version-only change never repairs. alsoChanges declares extra effects:
        Entity.all, Entity.where (answers whose conditions could overlap) or Entity.fields(key,
        fields), whose fields become unconfirmed: a confirming read is scheduled and lists repair
        only where they are condition or order fields. listen(stream).as([Entities], mapping) may
        change only those entities, mapping to Entity.delta(key, fields) (a partial change, a type
        error without the version field), Entity.row(value) (a whole row, where an omitted field
        is absent) or Entity.delete; a decoded delta that still lacks its version is dropped with
        UserClosure. A read's .one answer is whole in row's sense. Call-site optimistic, setData
        and clear are rejected on domain operations; writes may only invalidate. A domain write
        states key like every contract; flow check warns when a write that predicts exactly one
        record declares key fields that are not the input fields its predicted record's key reads
        (a many write, or one predicting several records or none, is exempt).

        One record per entity key holds received fields, each with its own version and position.
        Base is server-confirmed data; visible is base plus live predictions replayed over each
        new base: each field's function is called over the record beneath, a write's patches in
        written order, all fields of one patch over the same record; a field's function is called
        only over a record the store holds, and a patch over an absent record shows nothing, stays
        with its Attempt and is called when a base arrives; a value that throws or that its Schema
        rejects is uncomputed (the field shows its value beneath and the record reads predicted); a
        prediction fault behaves the same and is reported. Predictions are kept per entity, key and
        field. The judge: an answer with a lower version than the record's is silently stale, with
        Stale evidence; a higher version applies whenever it was asked, unless a version-less claim
        is open; equality is Schema.toEquivalence per field from entity({ fields }), else
        structural; by position, a field from a lookup that started before the stored value landed
        loses. Any response fills an absent field. A differing field that cannot be ordered is
        contested: its loader and holders refetch (or are only marked stale when the contest came
        from a repair's response) and the contest is recorded; Flow never merges. Predictions
        change visibility only and never start repairs. Two increments stay two increments; a later
        confirmed write of a field retires older predictions of it, per record.

        Each base change is matched against every active answer in the App, whichever domain
        declared it, on each side: a record that does not exist is a definite no, and a first
        sighting is no for a list and maybe for a derived answer; an answer repairs when the
        record's existence changed or a path its condition reads changed (segment arrays matched by
        prefix, arrays atomic, carried paths), unless the condition gives the same definite answer
        before and after. A list load that reveals records never held refetches derived answers on
        that entity once. A source rejected by ordering or a tombstone still joins derived answers,
        from its values to the current base; from a repair's response it only marks them stale.
        Repair rules: a one answer repairs only on delete; an ordered list repairs when a record
        enters or leaves or an order field of a record in it changes; an unordered list only on
        enter or leave; a server-ranked list also on any field change except unaffectedBy fields; a
        derived answer on enter or leave or a relevant field change. Merely displaying a field
        never needs repair. alsoChanges repairs every covered answer. A stream gap (an unproven or
        reset registration, an end or a release), restore and invalidate refetch covered answers
        that have subscribers, in canonical order, and mark the rest stale. A response behind the
        store for a record (a lower version) puts its answer out of date: from a machine's request
        the answer refetches; from a repair it is marked stale. A repair response updates copies
        and marks answers stale but never starts another lookup, so repair never cascades; the next
        machine request refreshes them. A request that started before an affecting change is stale
        on arrival. Each answer has one repair in flight and one trailing refetch. A settled write
        repairs for both the before-prediction and the at-settlement base, so an intervening push
        is never lost. Deltas are consolidated per record per turn but causes are never dropped.

        Write endings follow the Attempt's ending, never the send's channel (a definite failure
        after an unknown send of the same Attempt is StillUnknown, an unknown). At
        settlement Flow applies the ending to the store and releases record queues in one
        publication, then queues the ending, so store effects never depend on whether it routes
        current or late. Success installs response fields and ends confirmed predictions in one
        publication, so a value older than a settled write is never shown. A predicted slot (shown,
        uncomputed or faulted) whose whole value the response does not carry for that record stays
        unconfirmed until a confirming read (a later accepted read that carries the field) ends it
        even at an equal value. Settlement schedules one: an affected answer carrying the field
        that is already refetching, else the loader, else the first active answer carrying it, in
        canonical order. A read that omits the record confirms nothing, removes it from that answer
        and schedules the next candidate; a failed confirming read leaves the field unconfirmed
        until the next accepted read carrying it. If no active answer carries the field, the
        prediction ends and the field is dropped so the next reader fetches. A definite failure
        removes predictions without repair. Every NotSent (Rejected, Overloaded, Cancelled,
        Restored, StorageRefused, Disposed, NoPersistence, OldVersion, Unencodable, ModuleDisabled)
        and the evidence Superseded remove or never apply them, without repair. Unknown (the
        author's, Cancelled, Restored, Interrupted, Defect, StillUnknown) keeps live predictions
        until a forced repair lands, holds the record queue until that repair settles, and marks
        the record uncertain for that Attempt. A write restored as uncertain keeps its predicted
        keys with its Attempt, so restore marks them and forces the repair; the store hears the
        parked reason at the restore. An unknown that is not retained (bare, ignored, or a
        cancel's turn end) is Released: a release before the forced repair removes nothing, its
        predictions end with the repair, and an unknown never re-sent frees its queue entry at once.

        The forced repair runs with no timer: a failed repair is asked again, at most 3 asks per
        overdue interval, then RepairOverdue (proposed name; evidence; the mark and the hold stay);
        the count resets at RepairOverdue and on a placed answer, and news asks again (a new unknown
        on the record, a re-send's StillUnknown included, a release that leaves its mark, an answer
        the store cannot place, a newer value landing past a running repair, a reload's restored
        unknown); news during a running ask joins it (the runner's rulings). While a sent unknown's
        repair runs, RecordHeld evidence and Inspection's stalled: "record" (proposed names) name
        the wait. Per record, the queue is released once that record's forced repair settles; the
        held write keeps its lane hold and blocks no other actor's write to the record; its re-send
        waits behind whatever holds the record then and holds it again once sent; a restored unknown
        holds its record until the boot's repair settles. An unbound sent hold yields, with no
        clock, once a ready write waits behind it on a record: it is released, its ending unknown {
        Abandoned } (proposed reason) is parked for whoever adopts the identity, and its mark stays.

        Marks. uncertain is a mark per record and Attempt; a read, a forced repair, a failure and
        StillUnknown clear nothing, and neither time nor a later write's success does. A mark ends
        only by its own Attempt's success (a re-send) or, once given up, by guarded's proof. Given
        up (a release, an ignored unknown, a yield), it is uncertain and asks one read; a read or
        repair asked at or after the give-up, or a later success (by landing order) that carried
        every field of the mark, makes it quiet: unconfirmed on the fields its prediction touched,
        asking nothing, never uncertain again; a quiet removal doubt over a row a read shows present
        reads unconfirmed on every non-key field. A held mark is never demoted; a frame demotes
        nothing. guarded: true on the contract ends a quiet mark on an answer (read, repair, frame,
        any write's success) showing the target record's version strictly past the base the write
        was sent with, on the write's targets only, or on a later success of the same contract with
        the same business-key values; an equal version, a refusal or a held mark proves nothing.
        GuardContradicted (internal evidence, proposed name) voids the claim for the Runtime when a
        first send succeeds over a base the store already knew was stale; flow check reports
        GuardedUnbound, GuardedWithoutVersion and GuardedWithoutTarget (proposed names). A live
        .covers session with no open gap whose filter reads no field the write changes hides a
        given-up doubt without ending it; a removal is never hidden. At most 4,096 given-up marks
        per entity; past it the oldest given up is evicted with UnsettledEvicted (proposed name)
        evidence; an unresolved mark is never evicted. A mark holds its record, as a prediction
        does.

        Re-sending is O.x.retry(k?) only, in Held: the Attempt's input and basedOn unchanged, no
        prediction, joining the record queue, its response positioned at the Attempt's first send
        (a differing field it loses on is contested). basedOn gives each prediction's base version
        for If-Match. A never-sent queued write's basedOn rebases along the record queue only
        across a write ahead that was minted earlier over the same base; a stale success rebases
        nothing; a sent write is never rebased. Persisted actors acknowledge the Attempt write,
        with its predicted keys and basedOn, before the adapter runs (@plan.Persistence.O6).

        Predicting writes queue per record across actors, under actor concurrency (cancel
        concurrency is a flow check error; the O.x.cancel action acts by the lane table). The actor
        decides whether a start runs; the record queue decides when it is sent. A domain write
        joins its record queues when its Attempt heads its lane, including while its
        acknowledgement is out, never at mint or commit; a commit queued in its lane is a draft
        that predicts and holds no queue place. Per record, writes go in send order; across actors
        commit order holds only through the server's version check, so on a last-writer-wins source
        an earlier draft may overwrite a later commit (a stated limit, like two tabs). A
        multi-record write runs only when first in every queue it joined. A replaced never-sent
        write is evidence Superseded and a cancelled one ends NotSent { Cancelled }, both without
        repair. A store module's defect fails closed: a write that would join declines NotSent {
        ModuleDisabled } (proposed name) until reload.

        No temporary ids: a pending create is a memory draft the view shows apart from the answer;
        creates return the record and repair matching answers, and membership appears only when an
        answer's refetch holds its key (no optimistic inserts). A predicted move changes fields at
        once but keeps list membership until repair. A predicted delete hides the record
        everywhere; failure restores it. A confirmed delete leaves a tombstone while lookups started
        before it are in flight, so they cannot resurrect it. A versioned delete keeps it until the
        record's gcTime and a save at a lower or equal version cannot bring the record back; flow
        check warns on listen over an unversioned entity. A non-list reference to a deleted record
        reads a typed Deleted value. A read's own failure (never a defect or an interruption)
        reaches the store at the read's position, so a .gone(tag) read removes its key's row as a
        tombstone judged like any read; the machine hears the failure unchanged.

        listen: a stream entry whose values are record changes, with no bookmark. With no version
        on either side, a frame differing from an unechoed write is doubted, not applied, until a
        read started after it or a matching frame; with no write unechoed, a late unversioned frame
        is applied (a stated limit). listen(c).covers(pred), in eq terms printed by flow explain,
        says what a session watches; by default it covers the records the session delivered. Only
        an unproven registration marks: every non-key field of each covered record reads
        unconfirmed until a read or repair asked after it lands, and also records that may be in
        the cover by base, prediction or a filter field in doubt. A collection-wide version has no
        spelling.

        Records are held while an answer, a loader subscription or a prediction needs them. Only
        confirmed data persists; no prediction is persisted, and restored data never overrides live
        truth. Answer reads are resource lanes plus stale; a failed repair keeps data as a failed
        refresh. Record reads are { data, predicted, uncertain, unconfirmed }. value outcomes see
        confirmed data only, so a prediction never fires one and saved memory never captures one
        (@plan.Operations.O2). A read whose mapper throws or returns something with no shape ends on
        failure as Defect, with UserClosure.
        Reference: references/dispatch-desk/features/Tickets/ticket.domain.ts.

        Superseded: "Conditions use where (eq, oneOf, contains, between, overlaps, and)" is
        replaced by the operator list with yes, no or maybe; "An absent optional input drops its
        constraint." by the Type-side input rule (merge 5, 6.4). "predicts runs again at write
        start with the real input." and "conditionals, short-circuits and impure calls fail
        construction or flow check" are replaced by recorded positions, field functions and the
        allowlist lint; the construction-time check goes (merge 5). "Changes: predicts returns
        Entity.patch(key, fields) ..." gains field functions and many keys (merge 5) and key
        values (C115); listen maps to delta or row (C198). "on its old or new values; a missing
        condition field matches." is replaced by existence-aware matching (merge 5).
        "Reconnect, restore and invalidate" reads stream gaps (C99). "Predicted fields the response
        omits stay pending" becomes a predicted slot staying unconfirmed (merge 5; C27). "Failure
        or defect removes predictions and repairs affected answers, because a failed call may have
        partly applied." is replaced by "a definite failure removes predictions without repair"
        (C4). "Uncertain (cancelled after invocation, response lost, or restored mid-flight)
        forces the loader and every holder to refetch, keeps predictions until that repair lands,
        then shows server state." is restated as the unknown row: live predictions kept until a
        forced repair lands, the record queue held until it settles (C4; C189). "Rejection or
        cancellation before the adapter ran removes predictions without repair."
        becomes the NotSent list (C24). "The record stays uncertain until a later write to it
        succeeds or the machine reconciles the Attempt." is replaced by the mark rules (C25; C192).
        "unknown delivers an Attempt; O.x.retry(select) reissues that exact Attempt and a
        known-settled attempt settles without calling the adapter." is replaced by retry(k?) in
        Held (C23). "Predicting writes queue per record, FIFO across actors" is replaced by joining
        at the head of the lane in send order (C24; C187). "predictions show while queued" holds
        for drafts (C187). "Cancelling a queued, never-invoked write removes it." becomes NotSent {
        Cancelled } without repair (C24). "An uncertain write releases the next only after its
        forced repair settles, whether it succeeds or fails; the record stays uncertain." is
        amended per record with the repair bound (C189; C190; C207). "Creates return the record
        and repair matching answers; membership appears only when they refetch (no temporary ids
        or optimistic inserts)." is named as the draft pattern (C26). "Record reads are { data,
        pending, uncertain, unconfirmed }." is replaced by predicted (C27). The judge, guarded, the
        cap, ModuleDisabled, gone, listen and Released are added (C113; C169; C191; C193-C198;
        C208; C67).
    O2  Snapshot reads and evidence
        Entity.get(snapshot, key, shape) reads exactly that cut: requested fields with data and
        status, missing fields marked missing, undefined if the record is absent. Domain.entry.get
        returns the lane plus stale and ordered pages, or undefined if no active answer exists. A
        Story checkpoint reads a record with cp.entity(...) (@plan.Testing.O4), returning { data,
        predicted, uncertain, unconfirmed }. Record-queue changes are queue evidence records with
        their reason (Superseded, Cancelled, Released); an Overloaded write never joined and is an
        ending record. Reads never touch live state, advance time or start work.

        Every checkpoint and run end carries repairs and skips for considered answers, plus
        contests, HiddenOrder, prediction faults, Stale, RecordHeld, RepairOverdue,
        UnsettledEvicted, GuardContradicted and self-check findings, in the canonical order of
        @O5. uncertain's end reads "a later write succeeded, or a retry of the Attempt succeeded"
        as amended by the mark rules of @O1, and held lanes, bound or unbound, appear in
        Inspection by identity. In-process values, sensitive fields included, are faithful;
        exported evidence replaces sensitive values with keyed digests (@plan.Inspection.O5).
        Explaining a domain runs no adapter and uses the same repair classifier as the runtime
        (@plan.CommandLine.O1).
        Reference: references/dispatch-desk/features/Tickets/queue/queue.story.ts.

        Superseded: "Entity.get(snapshotOrCheckpoint, key, shape)" reads snapshots; checkpoints use
        cp.entity (C72). "Entity.queue returns ordered attempts and queue states." is replaced by
        queue records with their reason (C30; C84).
    O3  Hydration and lazy domains
        Hydrated confirmed fields keep their versions; positions and frontiers stay local to each
        Runtime. Predictions, queues, tombstones and uncertain marks never transfer, and sensitive
        fields are omitted (@plan.ServerRendering.O3). Absent fields are filled. Merge precedence
        for fields, answers and predictions is @plan.ServerRendering.O5. Hydration never fires an
        initial value event.

        Joining a lazy domain happens at admission, before any turn sees it: ids, entity objects,
        registering domains and byKey loaders are checked against the adopted graph, structure and
        restored data publish as one cut, and failure rolls back everything
        (@plan.LazyFeatures.O2). A loader in an inactive entry is unavailable, so a field with no
        carrier is dropped; flow check warns.
    O4  Property tests and audit mode
        propertyTest(story, domainPropertyOptions(domains, inputs)) (@plan.Testing.O6) generates
        ordinary static Stories against fresh Runtimes: successful, failed and uncertain writes;
        reordered, duplicated, failed and empty reads; pushes; stream faults; reconnect, restore,
        unsubscribe and invalidate; hydrate and mid-run domain adoption. The fixture oracle holds
        its own server state, not the domain's rules. At every publication it checks: copies of a
        field agree; at quiet, active answers equal the oracle in declared order or are stale with
        causes; no repair-response lookup and every lookup has causes; no accepted field regresses;
        every displayed unconfirmed value is a live prediction with a scheduled or failed
        confirming read; a record leaves an answer only with a recorded reason; identical replays
        give identical traces; every repair, skip and hidden record has causes. Quietness reads a
        recurring schedule as never quiet. Results are pass, fail or inconclusive, never
        inconclusive as success. Failures shrink to literal Stories with the seed.

        Audit is an explicit development and test option, off by default and never in production.
        After each settlement, push or observed change it refetches every active answer that was
        not repaired and compares without publishing: HiddenFilter reports a membership difference,
        UndeclaredEffect names the write, read and missing alsoChanges.

        Superseded: "propertyTest(domains, options)" is replaced by the domain form
        domainPropertyOptions(domains, inputs), keeping the oracle, quietness and inconclusive
        rules (C73; C84); stream faults are added (C99).
    O5  Bounds
        At most repairSlots repairs run at once (default 6, @plan.Runtime.O2), started in
        canonical order: delta, domain entry order, input key, then stable domain id. A paged
        repair of N loaded pages is N sequential requests in one slot. Each record queues at most
        64 waiting writes, drafts counted; a commit past any record queue, or a many key array past
        4,096 items (counted from the write's targets before any join, repeats included), ends
        NotSent { Overloaded } for that write, with no prediction and no partial join, and the
        transition and its other actions run. Recorded constants and persisted data obey the
        structural limits of @plan.Inspection.O2. Production has no cross-turn execution budget.

        Superseded: "if any record of a multi-record write would overflow, the whole write is
        rejected before any prediction, queue join or adapter work, with AdmissionOverloaded naming
        the record and limit" is replaced by NotSent { Overloaded } for that write (C22; C199).
decisions
    D1  Domains own shared-data policy
        Predictions replace call-site optimistic and setData; clear is forbidden and writes allow
        invalidate only.
constraints
    C1  Never invent server answers
        No predicted list membership, instant inserts, offline completeness, client-built query
        results, arbitrary predicate where, field merge, automatic uncertain retry or
        minimum-refetch promise. Machines own uncertain workflow.
    C2  Isolation
        One Runtime per scope and request; positions and frontiers never cross Runtimes; no
        prediction is persisted or hydrated; no version ever regresses.
verification
    V1  Authoring and types: unannotated inference of the reference domains; every rejected form
        (partial order, version in condition or patch, unused input, bad paging, undeclared patch
        entity, call-site optimistic) fails; the conformance table passes as the evaluator's
        golden test; the join rule's counterexamples (a field not carried, an unread field, a
        definite transition, a create, a delete, a first sighting, no condition, a nested path, a
        Schema default) each have a test; two-record settlement has a test; freezing and
        checkReplays catch a mutation, Math.random and a second-call throw; lint goldens.
    V2  Repair properties: oracle agreement, coherence, no cascade and monotonic truth hold under
        propertyTest, and removing any one repair rule makes a named variant fail.
    V3  Prediction endings: confirming read (equal value, missing record, no carrier), definite
        failure without repair, retirement of older predictions, unknown with its forced repair and
        its bound, mark demotion and guarded proof, and the unversioned tombstone guard each have a
        targeted test.
    V4  Evidence: checkpoint reads, faithful in-process and digested export values, and impact
        tables agree with observed repairs; audit catches an omitted filter and an undeclared
        effect.
    V5  Hydrate, lazy join and bounds: A-to-B hydrate merges amid client writes, lazy adoption
        with rollback, 6 repair slots, N-request paged chains, and the 64/65 queue boundary with
        no partial joins.

        Superseded: V1's "source check rejects conditionals and impurity" (merge 5, 6.4); V3's
        "partial failure repair" (C4).
questions
    Q1  Are 6 repair slots right?
        Recommended: measure Dispatch Desk page churn before changing the default.
    Q2  Deferred to build time
        Per-turn counters and exact event field lists; index structure for matching; tombstone gc
        accounting; evidence retention and sequence headroom; oracle evidence handoff.
